{"kind":"assert","text":"Independent read-only preflight of the revised design at b26dd2936bebf529b9ae38c545482c220a791930, tree d9a23c58174e806a6d27148d9cdcd6f3452b8d47. Existing clone/version design ownership remains. This is feedback before the normal successor evidence binding and invitation; it is not a guarded verdict, adoption, source approval or partial implementation closure.\n\nRoot personally read the complete 32,038-byte design and complete 29,496-byte native U3 against 5fa, through both EOFs in nine bounded displays. The two independent reviewers also read both units completely at this new head. No predecessor body credit was used. Native metadata confirms that only this note changed. Body SHA256: 129f05f1e70cec817fbabcdd3c1ea8aaa132afc0903241bf4387df13234eb07f. U3 SHA256: a720a7efda45190ac056a15d61192d732b18ce6e626883424e7d1540c337a777. Root audit and all three actual ledgers are retained in the b26 scratch directory; total unique subject coverage is 61,534 bytes and 799 LF, not tripled by independent readers.\n\nArchitecture and security: the amendments materially answer the earlier 7229 decisions. V1a separates pure historical judgment from current read, admission, standing and provider guards, with conjunctive precedence and no archived session code replacing current strict checks. Operator bootstrap is an explicitly new, narrow read-only proposal with pinned identity, complete scope allowlist, purpose, signature/address/expiry/replay bounds and no custody or tokens; it requires owner adoption and does not authenticate itself through unresolved history. V2 distinguishes stable service, immutable admission release and current runtime release. Canonical signed registry revisions, operator anchors, rotation/reset, freshness floors, local trust acceptance and exact V4 witnessed-head coverage are explicit. Replay success and publication signatures do not substitute for actual build/source evidence. Later coverage needs signed exact-head evidence; mixed histories remain unresolved rather than silently switched.\n\nH3 now persists the original attempt's full scope/incarnation, origin, operation/attempt, bundle, binding content ID, exact provider identity and capability/ref/lifetime limits. Dispatch, recovery, replies, credential retrieval and cleanup follow that original record across restart and revision changes. Unknown old attribution stays held; secret rotation cannot expand rights. H4 names the coherent runtime/registry/routing/bundle tuple, durable active pointer, per-operation convergence, and a durable issuance/draining/finalization barrier. Abort records preclude later admission and duplicate reopening. Missing responses, leases and timeouts do not retire tickets. These address the prior cached-object and original-binding ambiguities.\n\nOne precise clarification remains before adopting the physical-send barrier. A logical send-start mark can finalize and retire an old-generation ticket while its dispatcher has not entered the provider call. The note then requires a new ticket if activation changed before dispatch, but leaves the ownership transition between final generation check, ticket retirement and provider entry unspecified. A proposed state sequence is: old ticket commits its logical mark; coordinator authenticates and retires it; activation switches to a compatible tuple; the old continuation is still suspended before its provider call. Name what makes that continuation irreversibly responsible for the original attempt, what prevents incorrect resumption after the switch, and when dispatch is conservatively classified as may-have-started. The candidate's preservation of the attempt duty does not alone define those transitions.\n\nChoose an explicit invariant in the same owner protocol: keep the permit outstanding through irrevocable dispatcher responsibility, or use an authenticated single-owner transfer that prevents old resumption, or deliberately allow a compatible old continuation under defined current checks and original-attempt rules. State when abort remains possible and when uncertainty stays permanently with that attempt. This is a pre-adoption protocol amendment or explicitly held owner proof obligation, not a demonstrated deployed bug. It does not demand an atomic provider RPC transaction, completion of every remote outcome, a second effect engine, invented instant revocation, new tests now or another task/matrix. The design already identifies the physical boundary as proof owed; making its decisive state explicit makes that proof reviewable.\n\nScalability and simplification: a small exact root list and narrow evaluator/attempt interfaces are useful. The new coordinator lies on admission and send-start paths; root count alone does not bound its load. Existing capacity work should account for issue/finalize transactions, peak outstanding permits, authenticated recovery backlog, retained abort/ticket exclusion and old/new runtime overlap, as well as registry/reference-closure/archive bytes. Backpressure may refuse new work, never retire issued tickets merely to meet a bound. Sixteen roots remains a candidate, not an adopted or measured limit. Determine the actual consistency domain before any sharding; avoid weakening the barrier in pursuit of throughput. Preserve all functional scope and old duties regardless of the demo estimates.\n\nRemaining evidence is honestly open: authentic retained old/new histories, full scope/incarnation/genesis/head/build/source receipts, compatible port correspondence, exact bootstrap/transaction schemas, numeric work bounds, operator/security/contract adoption and implementation witnesses. The revision supplies decisions to review; it does not prove those receipts or an operational deployment. The failure and rollback rules preserve records, old roots and custody and use a new monotonic generation rather than lowering the registry floor. No fresh room substitutes for old-room support.\n\nActivity was limited to immutable native Git, static note/report reading, raw SHA/count/JSON checks and scratch evidence. No project execution/import, generated histories or vectors, tests/gate/compiler/install, provider/private/process/browser probes, source edits, landing or adoption. Root owns the independent assessment, lead owns order and existing producers own implementation. Live operations separately now inspects satisfied and terminal landed under receipt 4a6d7023; that closure lends no design authority here.\n"}